<ReportItem port="3389" svc_name="msrdp" protocol="tcp" severity="0" pluginID="121010" pluginName="TLS Version 1.1 Protocol Detection" pluginFamily="Service detection">
<asset_inventory>True</asset_inventory>
<description>The remote service accepts connections encrypted using TLS 1.1.
TLS 1.1 lacks support for current and recommended cipher suites.
Ciphers that support encryption before MAC computation, and authenticated encryption modes such as GCM cannot be used with TLS 1.1

As of March 31, 2020, Endpoints that are not enabled for TLS 1.2 and higher will no longer function properly with major web browsers and major vendors.</description>
<fname>tls11_detection.nasl</fname>
<plugin_modification_date>2020/08/07</plugin_modification_date>
<plugin_name>TLS Version 1.1 Protocol Detection</plugin_name>
<plugin_publication_date>2019/01/08</plugin_publication_date>
<plugin_type>remote</plugin_type>
<risk_factor>None</risk_factor>
<script_version>1.9</script_version>
<see_also>https://tools.ietf.org/html/draft-ietf-tls-oldversions-deprecate-00
http://www.nessus.org/u?c8ae820d</see_also>
<solution>Enable support for TLS 1.2 and/or 1.3, and disable support for TLS 1.1.</solution>
<synopsis>The remote service encrypts traffic using an older version of TLS.</synopsis>
<plugin_output>TLSv1.1 is enabled and the server supports at least one cipher.</plugin_output>
</ReportItem>
<ReportItem port="3389" svc_name="msrdp" protocol="tcp" severity="2" pluginID="104743" pluginName="TLS Version 1.0 Protocol Detection" pluginFamily="Service detection">
<asset_inventory>True</asset_inventory>
<cvss3_base_score>6.5</cvss3_base_score>
<cvss3_vector>CVSS:3.0/AV:N/AC:H/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:L/A:N</cvss3_vector>
<cvss_base_score>6.1</cvss_base_score>
<cvss_score_rationale>score from a more in depth analysis done by Tenable</cvss_score_rationale>
<cvss_score_source>manual</cvss_score_source>
<cvss_vector>CVSS2#AV:N/AC:H/Au:N/C:C/I:P/A:N</cvss_vector>
<description>The remote service accepts connections encrypted using TLS 1.0. TLS 1.0 has a number of cryptographic design flaws. Modern implementations of TLS 1.0 mitigate these problems, but newer versions of TLS like 1.2 and 1.3 are designed against these flaws and should be used whenever possible.

As of March 31, 2020, Endpoints that aren’t enabled for TLS 1.2 and higher will no longer function properly with major web browsers and major vendors.

PCI DSS v3.2 requires that TLS 1.0 be disabled entirely by June 30, 2018, except for POS POI terminals (and the SSL/TLS termination points to which they connect) that can be verified as not being susceptible to any known exploits.</description>
<fname>tls10_detection.nasl</fname>
<plugin_modification_date>2020/03/31</plugin_modification_date>
<plugin_name>TLS Version 1.0 Protocol Detection</plugin_name>
<plugin_publication_date>2017/11/22</plugin_publication_date>
<plugin_type>remote</plugin_type>
<risk_factor>Medium</risk_factor>
<script_version>1.9</script_version>
<see_also>https://tools.ietf.org/html/draft-ietf-tls-oldversions-deprecate-00</see_also>
<solution>Enable support for TLS 1.2 and 1.3, and disable support for TLS 1.0.</solution>
<synopsis>The remote service encrypts traffic using an older version of TLS.</synopsis>
<plugin_output>TLSv1 is enabled and the server supports at least one cipher.</plugin_output>
</ReportItem>
